# Native config limits

Each rule below exists because it broke AR attempt #1. See
`docs/ar-postmortem.md` RC-4 and RC-5.

## Never disable debugging to smooth AR tracking

```kotlin
// ❌ BAD — breaks the debugger repo-wide, for every feature, forever (RC-5)
buildTypes {
    debug { isDebuggable = false }
}
```

Tracking-smoothness work belongs in `profile` / `release`.

## Never let a dependency pull the toolchain forward

```kotlin
// ❌ BAD — all four are independent build-break vectors (RC-4)
compileSdk = 37                                      // ahead of Flutter stable
implementation("com.google.ar:core:1.52.0")          // races the plugin's own
id("org.gradle.toolchains.foojay-resolver-convention") version "1.0.0"
```

```properties
# ❌ BAD — silent JDK provisioning
org.gradle.java.installations.auto-download=true
```

```kotlin
// ✅ GOOD — track the Flutter template
compileSdk = flutter.compileSdkVersion
minSdk = 24   // ARCore floor: the one deliberate override
```

Let the AR plugin supply its own ARCore version transitively.

## Allowed AR config

`minSdk = 24` · `CAMERA` permission · `com.google.ar.core` meta-data set to
`optional` · `com.google.ar.core` in `<queries>`.

Anything beyond that list needs a constitution amendment.

## Pin AR plugin versions exactly

```yaml
ar_flutter_plugin_plus: 1.1.3   # ✅ exact — no caret
ar_flutter_plugin_plus: ^1.1.3  # ❌ a silent minor bump mid-demo
```

## One concern per commit

Dependency additions and native/Gradle edits are **separate commits**, each
stating the reason and the rollback.
